 smartphone digital or optical zoom. The zoom should not be perfectly smooth. Include tiny natural corrections, subtle hand tremor, minor autofocus changes, and slight exposure adjustments.\\n\\nMove into a tighter rear shot focused on the upper body, back, clothing, hands, or the important detail.\\n\\nMomentarily zoom closer to the interesting detail, as if the filmer is trying to see it more clearly.\\n\\nHold the close-up briefly.\\n\\nThen slightly zoom back out to re-establish the subject in the environment.\\n\\nChange naturally to a rear three-quarter angle by taking one or two small steps sideways.\\n\\nUse an occasional side profile angle when appropriate, but never move directly in front of the subject.\\n\\nAt another moment, film from slightly lower chest height, creating the feeling that the phone is being held discreetly near the filmer’s body.\\n\\nAnother shot may be taken from behind a nearby object such as another person, a chair, a tree, a parked vehicle, a doorway, a shelf, or street furniture. The foreground object can partially cover the frame for a moment.\\n\\nOccasionally allow a passerby to briefly walk between the camera and the subject.\\n\\nUse realistic zoom behavior:\\nwide view → medium zoom → close zoom → slight zoom out → reframe → zoom toward another interesting detail.\\n\\nThe filmer should sometimes hesitate before zooming, make small framing mistakes, briefly lose perfect focus, then correct the framing naturally.\\n\\nCAMERA FEEL:\\n\\nordinary smartphone camera,\\nnatural handheld movement,\\nsmall hand tremors,\\nslight breathing movement,\\noccasional micro-jitters,\\nminor rolling shutter,\\nsubtle autofocus hunting,\\nsmall exposure changes,\\nnatural motion blur,\\nrealistic smartphone sharpening,\\nslightly imperfect framing,\\nno professional stabilization.\\n\\nThe footage should feel spontaneous and unplanned.\\n\\nThe person filming should behave as if they are trying not to attract attention. Camera movement should be cautious and subtle rather than aggressive.\\n\\nDifferent scenes may use different filming distances:\\n\\nfar candid observation from 8–15 meters away,\\n\\nmedium observation from 4–8 meters,\\n\\nclose observation from 2–4 meters,\\n\\nbrief zoomed-in detail captured from a safe distance.\\n\\nVary the apparent filming position naturally:\\n\\nstanding behind the subject,\\n\\nsitting behind the subject,\\n\\nfilming from across a walkway,\\n\\nfilming from another table,\\n\\nfilming through a gap between people,\\n\\nfilming from behind a parked car,\\n\\nfilming from behind a bench,\\n\\nfilming from the opposite side of a room,\\n\\nfilming while slowly walking behind the subject.\\n\\nThe subject’s behavior must remain natural for their age.\\n\\nFor a young adult, use confident but ordinary body movement.\\n\\nFor a middle-aged subject, use realistic posture and natural pacing.\\n\\nFor an elderly person, use believable slower movements, realistic posture, natural walking speed, and age-appropriate body language without exaggeration.\\n\\nFor younger subjects, keep all actions ordinary, safe, non-sexualized, and appropriate for their age.\\n\\nBACKGROUND:\\n\\nPeople around the subject continue normal activities.\\n\\nPedestrians walk naturally.\\n\\nPeople talk, sit, eat, use their phones, wait, shop, or pass by.\\n\\nNobody intentionally looks into the camera.\\n\\nDo not make background people freeze.\\n\\nUse natural environmental movement such as wind moving clothing, hair, tree leaves, curtains, traffic, shadows, or nearby objects.\\n\\nLIGHTING:\\n\\nnatural daylight or realistic indoor available light,\\n\\nordinary phone-camera dynamic range,\\n\\nrealistic shadows,\\n\\nrealistic skin texture,\\n\\nnatural clothing texture,\\n\\nno artificial studio lighting.\\n\\nThe final result should look like a real person discree\",null,null,null,\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/asb/ANqvLOYVgse5IKN4zYDKsFG8s2Zn9kZoBT1MZRcS4hrgZeD-Bi-toQDhI5YmyshrPN4